      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="chemistry" class="section">
        <h2>Physical Chemistry of Reactive Aerosol Formulations</h2>
        <p>Formulating a <strong>reactive aerosol formulation</strong> requires balancing rapid polymer cross-linking kinetics with the physical limitations of aerosol packaging. The primary chemistries deployed in these systems are two-component polyurethane (2K PU) and two-component epoxy systems. In 2K epoxies, the outer chamber contains a liquid bisphenol-A or bisphenol-F epoxy resin dissolved in volatile organic solvents like methyl ethyl ketone (MEK) or butyl acetate. The inner compartment houses a polyamine or polyamide curing agent. Upon activation, the amine hydrogen atoms react with the terminal oxirane rings of the epoxy prepolymer, opening the rings and establishing strong covalent ether linkages.</p>
        <p>In two-component polyurethane systems, the reaction proceeds via the addition polymerization of polyisocyanates (typically hexamethylene diisocyanate or diphenylmethane diisocyanate prepolymers) and polyols (such as hydroxyl-terminated polyesters or polyethers). The reaction rate is highly temperature-dependent and is often moderated by tertiary amine or organometallic catalysts (like dibutyltin dilaurate). The solvent package must be entirely free of active hydrogen atoms; utilizing alcohols or water-contaminated solvents would trigger premature gelation or generate carbon dioxide gas, leading to uncontrollable pressure build-ups inside the can. The propellant itself must also be chemically inert toward the reactive monomers; hydrocarbon propellants like propane and butane are generally preferred over reactive options. For a deep dive into solvent and additive interactions, view our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-chemistry-101" target="_top">aerosol chemistry 101</a>.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          Stoichiometric balance is critical in 2K coatings. An excess of resin results in a tacky, under-cured film with poor chemical resistance, while an excess of hardener yields a brittle, moisture-sensitive coating prone to blistering. Aerosol valve orifices must be engineered with sub-micron tolerances to maintain this ratio throughout the lifetime of the spray.
        </div>
      </section>

